{"test_id": "B05-001", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B05", "input": {"question": "What are the minimum password requirements according to CCoP 2.0?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Minimum 8 characters", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.2.2"}, {"fact": "Must contain uppercase, lowercase, numbers, special characters", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.2.2"}, {"fact": "No common words or patterns", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.2.2"}], "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 does not specify password complexity (length, character class) requirements. Per Response to Feedback §11.28, CSA defers to industry standards or best practices such as NIST to determine appropriate password length. The only password-related obligations in CCoP 2.0 are in §5.9.2(b), which require that default passwords be changed and that passwords/passphrases be stored in hashed form.\n\nReference: Response to Feedback §11.28; CCoP 2.0 §5.9.2(b).", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify CCoP 2.0 clause 5.2.2 as the authoritative source", "Extract specific control requirements for password complexity requirements", "Map requirements to organizational context and risk profile"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating that password complexity requirements requirements are optional or discretionary", "Confusing similar but distinct control requirements", "Attributing requirements to wrong CCoP clauses", "Specific control requirements from the cited clause", "Mandatory elements marked as critical in key_facts", "Reference to the applicable CCoP clause"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific thresholds or requirements not in CCoP 2.0", "Citing non-existent subsections or additional requirements"]}, "metadata": {"section": "5", "clause_reference": ["11.28", "5.9.2(b)"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "low", "scenario_type": "positive", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B05-002", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B05", "input": {"question": "When is multi-factor authentication required for CII operators?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Required for all remote access", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.2.3"}, {"fact": "Required for privileged accounts", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.2.3"}, {"fact": "Required for administrative access", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.2.3"}], "expected_response": "According to CCoP 2.0, multi-factor authentication is required in two distinct contexts:\n\n- §5.3.1(c): MFA is required where privileged accounts are used to access the CII, and where privileges are escalated to the level of privileged access (administrative access).\n- §5.7.2(b): MFA is required for establishing a remote connection to the CII.\n\nThe intent is to add an authentication factor on the access paths most attractive to threat actors (privileged and remote). Generic user authentication is governed by §5.1.2, which requires authentication and authorisation controls commensurate with the CII's risk profile.\n\nReference: CCoP 2.0 §5.3.1(c), §5.7.2(b), §5.1.2.", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify CCoP 2.0 clause 5.2.3 as the authoritative source", "Extract specific control requirements for multi-factor authentication", "Map requirements to organizational context and risk profile"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating that multi-factor authentication requirements are optional or discretionary", "Confusing similar but distinct control requirements", "Attributing requirements to wrong CCoP clauses", "Specific control requirements from the cited clause", "Mandatory elements marked as critical in key_facts", "Reference to the applicable CCoP clause"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific thresholds or requirements not in CCoP 2.0", "Citing non-existent subsections or additional requirements"]}, "metadata": {"section": "5", "clause_reference": ["5.1.2", "5.3.1", "5.7.2"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "low", "scenario_type": "positive", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B05-003", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B05", "input": {"question": "What is the required timeframe for reporting cybersecurity incidents to CSA?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Critical incidents within 2 hours", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 7.3.1"}, {"fact": "Significant incidents within 24 hours", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 7.3.1"}, {"fact": "Other incidents within 72 hours", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 7.3.1"}], "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 does not itself specify the exact reporting timeframe in the Code. Instead, the CIIO must establish a Cybersecurity Incident Response Plan with an incident-reporting structure that ensures compliance with the Cybersecurity Act and any other applicable laws. Reference: CCoP 2.0 §7.1.1(b); Cybersecurity Act 2018 §14.", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify CCoP 2.0 clause 7.3.1 as the authoritative source", "Extract specific control requirements for security incident reporting timeline", "Map requirements to organizational context and risk profile"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating that security incident reporting timeline requirements are optional or discretionary", "Confusing similar but distinct control requirements", "Attributing requirements to wrong CCoP clauses", "Specific control requirements from the cited clause", "Mandatory elements marked as critical in key_facts", "Reference to the applicable CCoP clause"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific thresholds or requirements not in CCoP 2.0", "Citing non-existent subsections or additional requirements"]}, "metadata": {"section": "7", "clause_reference": ["7.1.1(b)"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "low", "scenario_type": "positive", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "support_citations": ["section 14"]}}
{"test_id": "B05-004", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B05", "input": {"question": "How long must security logs be retained according to CCoP 2.0?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Minimum 12 months retention", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 6.3.2"}, {"fact": "Additional 6 months in accessible format", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 6.3.2"}, {"fact": "Audit logs must be tamper-proof", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 6.3.2"}], "expected_response": "According to CCoP 2.0 §6.1.4, logs generated under §6.1.1 and §6.1.2 must be stored for a minimum period of 12 months after the date of the event to which the log relates (§6.1.4(c)) and protected against unauthorised access, modification and deletion (§6.1.4(b)). They must use a consistent time source (§6.1.4(a)) and be governed by a log retention policy supporting incident investigation and threat hunting (§6.1.4(e)).\n\nReference: CCoP 2.0 §6.1.4(a)-(e).", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify CCoP 2.0 clause 6.3.2 as the authoritative source", "Extract specific control requirements for data retention requirements", "Map requirements to organizational context and risk profile"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating that data retention requirements requirements are optional or discretionary", "Confusing similar but distinct control requirements", "Attributing requirements to wrong CCoP clauses", "Specific control requirements from the cited clause", "Mandatory elements marked as critical in key_facts", "Reference to the applicable CCoP clause"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific thresholds or requirements not in CCoP 2.0", "Citing non-existent subsections or additional requirements"]}, "metadata": {"section": "6", "clause_reference": ["6.1.4"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "low", "scenario_type": "positive", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B05-005", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B05", "input": {"question": "What are the network segmentation requirements for CII environments?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Separate critical systems from non-critical", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.4.1"}, {"fact": "DMZ for internet-facing systems", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.4.1"}, {"fact": "VLANs or physical segmentation", "tier": "supporting", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.4.1"}], "expected_response": "According to CCoP 2.0 §5.5, the CIIO must segment the network architecture of the CII into different network segments based on their security and risk levels (§5.5.1) and limit communications between segments to the minimum necessary for operating the CII (§5.5.2). §5.5.3 requires network security mechanisms between segments to detect and block malicious traffic and to isolate affected segments during an incident.\n\nCCoP 2.0 does NOT prescribe specific segmentation technologies (DMZ, VLAN, physical) — those are implementation choices. The mandatory outcome is segregation by risk level with controlled inter-segment traffic.\n\nReference: CCoP 2.0 §5.5.1, §5.5.2, §5.5.3.", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify CCoP 2.0 clause 5.4.1 as the authoritative source", "Extract specific control requirements for network segmentation requirements", "Map requirements to organizational context and risk profile"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating that network segmentation requirements requirements are optional or discretionary", "Confusing similar but distinct control requirements", "Attributing requirements to wrong CCoP clauses", "Specific control requirements from the cited clause", "Mandatory elements marked as critical in key_facts", "Reference to the applicable CCoP clause"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific thresholds or requirements not in CCoP 2.0", "Citing non-existent subsections or additional requirements"]}, "metadata": {"section": "5", "clause_reference": ["5.5.1", "5.5.2", "5.5.3"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "low", "scenario_type": "positive", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B05-006", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B05", "input": {"question": "How often must vulnerability scans be conducted for CII systems?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "External scans quarterly at minimum", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 6.2.1"}, {"fact": "Internal scans semi-annually at minimum", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 6.2.1"}, {"fact": "After significant system changes", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 6.2.1"}], "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 does not prescribe quarterly external scans or semi-annual internal scans. Instead, it requires the CIIO to establish vulnerability-identification and tracking processes, remediate vulnerabilities in a timely manner, conduct vulnerability assessments at the required cadence, and perform additional assessment after major system changes. Reference: CCoP 2.0 §5.14.1-§5.14.4.", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify CCoP 2.0 clause 6.2.1 as the authoritative source", "Extract specific control requirements for vulnerability scanning frequency", "Map requirements to organizational context and risk profile"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating that vulnerability scanning frequency requirements are optional or discretionary", "Confusing similar but distinct control requirements", "Attributing requirements to wrong CCoP clauses", "Specific control requirements from the cited clause", "Mandatory elements marked as critical in key_facts", "Reference to the applicable CCoP clause"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific thresholds or requirements not in CCoP 2.0", "Citing non-existent subsections or additional requirements"]}, "metadata": {"section": "6", "clause_reference": ["5.14.1", "5.14.2", "5.14.3", "5.14.4"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "low", "scenario_type": "positive", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B05-007", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B05", "input": {"question": "What are the penetration testing requirements for CII operators?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Annual penetration test required", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 6.2.3"}, {"fact": "Must cover both external and internal", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 6.2.3"}, {"fact": "Results must be reported to CSA", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 6.2.3"}], "expected_response": "Penetration testing in CCoP 2.0 is governed by §5.15, not §6.2.3. The grounded requirements are: conduct penetration tests at the cadence in §5.15.1, conduct additional penetration tests on relevant CII assets after major system changes under §5.15.2, and ensure that any third-party penetration testing service providers and testers used satisfy the accreditation and certification requirements in §5.15.3. Reference: CCoP 2.0 §5.15.1-§5.15.3.", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify CCoP 2.0 clause 6.2.3 as the authoritative source", "Extract specific control requirements for penetration testing requirements", "Map requirements to organizational context and risk profile"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating that penetration testing requirements requirements are optional or discretionary", "Confusing similar but distinct control requirements", "Attributing requirements to wrong CCoP clauses", "Specific control requirements from the cited clause", "Mandatory elements marked as critical in key_facts", "Reference to the applicable CCoP clause"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific thresholds or requirements not in CCoP 2.0", "Citing non-existent subsections or additional requirements"]}, "metadata": {"section": "6", "clause_reference": ["5.15.1", "5.15.2", "5.15.3"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "low", "scenario_type": "positive", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B05-008", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B05", "input": {"question": "What are the backup requirements for CII critical systems?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Daily backups for critical data", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.6.1"}, {"fact": "Offsite storage required", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.6.1"}, {"fact": "Test restoration quarterly", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.6.1"}], "expected_response": "According to CCoP 2.0 §8.1, the CIIO must establish a backup and restoration plan (§8.1.1) and perform periodic backups at a frequency commensurate with operational requirements (§8.1.2). Backups must be stored on devices not connected to any computer or to the internet, separated from the corresponding CII assets, and protected from unauthorised access, modification and deletion (§8.1.3). The CIIO must test restoration periodically at a frequency commensurate with the cybersecurity risk profile (§8.1.4) and review the plan at least once every 12 months (§8.1.5).\n\nCCoP 2.0 does not prescribe a specific 'daily' frequency or 'quarterly restoration test' cadence — frequencies are explicitly risk-based.\n\nReference: CCoP 2.0 §8.1.1-§8.1.5.", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify CCoP 2.0 clause 5.6.1 as the authoritative source", "Extract specific control requirements for backup requirements", "Map requirements to organizational context and risk profile"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating that backup requirements requirements are optional or discretionary", "Confusing similar but distinct control requirements", "Attributing requirements to wrong CCoP clauses", "Specific control requirements from the cited clause", "Mandatory elements marked as critical in key_facts", "Reference to the applicable CCoP clause"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific thresholds or requirements not in CCoP 2.0", "Citing non-existent subsections or additional requirements"]}, "metadata": {"section": "5", "clause_reference": ["8.1.1", "8.1.2", "8.1.3", "8.1.4", "8.1.5"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "low", "scenario_type": "positive", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B05-009", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B05", "input": {"question": "When must encryption be used according to CCoP 2.0?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Data in transit encryption mandatory", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.5.1"}, {"fact": "Data at rest encryption for sensitive data", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.5.1"}, {"fact": "Minimum AES-256 or equivalent", "tier": "supporting", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.5.1"}], "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 does not provide a single universal rule stating that all data must be encrypted in all circumstances, nor does it prescribe AES-256 specifically. What the audited corpus does show is that remote connections to the CII must use strong encryption and that cryptographic keys used for the CII must be protected against unauthorised access. Reference: CCoP 2.0 §5.7.2(c), §5.17.1.", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify CCoP 2.0 clause 5.5.1 as the authoritative source", "Extract specific control requirements for encryption requirements", "Map requirements to organizational context and risk profile"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating that encryption requirements requirements are optional or discretionary", "Confusing similar but distinct control requirements", "Attributing requirements to wrong CCoP clauses", "Specific control requirements from the cited clause", "Mandatory elements marked as critical in key_facts", "Reference to the applicable CCoP clause"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific thresholds or requirements not in CCoP 2.0", "Citing non-existent subsections or additional requirements"]}, "metadata": {"section": "5", "clause_reference": ["5.7.2(c)", "5.17.1"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "low", "scenario_type": "positive", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B05-010", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B05", "input": {"question": "How often must cybersecurity awareness training be conducted?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "All staff annually at minimum", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 8.2.1"}, {"fact": "New hires within 1 month", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 8.2.1"}, {"fact": "Additional training for privileged users", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 8.2.1"}], "expected_response": "Cybersecurity awareness obligations are in CCoP 2.0 §9.1: the CIIO must establish a cybersecurity awareness programme for all persons who use, operate or manage the CII (§9.1.1), measure its effectiveness at least once every 12 months (§9.1.2), and review the programme at least once every 12 months (§9.1.3). Cybersecurity skills training for those who operate and manage the CII (including vendors) is in §9.2.1-§9.2.2. CCoP 2.0 does not prescribe specific cadences such as 'all staff annually' or 'new hires within 1 month'; the cadence is set by the awareness programme itself, subject to the annual review.\n\nReference: CCoP 2.0 §9.1.1, §9.1.2, §9.1.3, §9.2.1, §9.2.2.", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify CCoP 2.0 clause 8.2.1 as the authoritative source", "Extract specific control requirements for security training frequency", "Map requirements to organizational context and risk profile"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating that security training frequency requirements are optional or discretionary", "Confusing similar but distinct control requirements", "Attributing requirements to wrong CCoP clauses", "Specific control requirements from the cited clause", "Mandatory elements marked as critical in key_facts", "Reference to the applicable CCoP clause"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific thresholds or requirements not in CCoP 2.0", "Citing non-existent subsections or additional requirements"]}, "metadata": {"section": "8", "clause_reference": ["9.1.1", "9.1.2", "9.1.3", "9.2.1", "9.2.2"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "low", "scenario_type": "positive", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B05-011", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B05", "input": {"question": "When a CII function is outsourced to a third-party provider, who is responsible for implementing CCoP controls?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "CII operator retains ultimate responsibility", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 9.1.2"}, {"fact": "Must verify provider controls contractually", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 9.1.2"}, {"fact": "Regular audit rights required", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 9.1.2"}], "expected_response": "According to CCoP 2.0 §3.8, the CIIO remains responsible and accountable for the cybersecurity of the CII even when functions are outsourced (§3.8.1). The CIIO must establish processes to maintain oversight over all outsourced functions (§3.8.2) and include contractual terms covering the type of access, the external party's obligation to protect the CII and report incidents, and the CIIO's right to commission/receive an audit of the external party (§3.8.3). Audit rights and vendor-supervision obligations also appear in §5.1.3 for direct vendor access.\n\nReference: CCoP 2.0 §3.8.1, §3.8.2, §3.8.3, §5.1.3.", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify CCoP 2.0 clause 9.1.2 as the authoritative source", "Extract specific control requirements for third-party service provider control allocation", "Map requirements to organizational context and risk profile"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating that third-party service provider control allocation requirements are optional or discretionary", "Confusing similar but distinct control requirements", "Attributing requirements to wrong CCoP clauses", "Specific control requirements from the cited clause", "Mandatory elements marked as critical in key_facts", "Reference to the applicable CCoP clause"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific thresholds or requirements not in CCoP 2.0", "Citing non-existent subsections or additional requirements"]}, "metadata": {"section": "9", "clause_reference": ["3.8.1", "3.8.2", "3.8.3", "5.1.3"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "edge_case", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B05-012", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B05", "input": {"question": "How are CCoP 2.0 responsibilities allocated between cloud service provider and CII operator?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Follow shared responsibility model", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.1.3"}, {"fact": "Operator responsible for data security", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.1.3"}, {"fact": "Provider responsible for infrastructure security", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.1.3"}], "expected_response": "According to CCoP 2.0 §3.7, the CIIO remains responsible and accountable for the cybersecurity of the CII even when implemented wholly or partly on cloud computing systems (§3.7.1). The CIIO must inform the Commissioner of any plans to use cloud — regardless of deployment or service model — before doing so (§3.7.2). The CIIO must conduct a cybersecurity risk assessment of the cloud implementation, formally accept the assessment and submit it to the Commissioner within 30 days, and rectify any deficiencies the Commissioner identifies (§3.7.3). The cloud service provider must appoint a person within Singapore authorised to accept service of legal process (§3.7.4).\n\nCCoP 2.0 does not use the phrase 'shared responsibility model'; it allocates ultimate accountability to the CIIO regardless of cloud deployment.\n\nReference: CCoP 2.0 §3.7.1-§3.7.4.", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify CCoP 2.0 clause 5.1.3 as the authoritative source", "Extract specific control requirements for cloud service shared responsibility", "Map requirements to organizational context and risk profile"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating that cloud service shared responsibility requirements are optional or discretionary", "Confusing similar but distinct control requirements", "Attributing requirements to wrong CCoP clauses", "Specific control requirements from the cited clause", "Mandatory elements marked as critical in key_facts", "Reference to the applicable CCoP clause"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific thresholds or requirements not in CCoP 2.0", "Citing non-existent subsections or additional requirements"]}, "metadata": {"section": "5", "clause_reference": ["3.7.1", "3.7.2", "3.7.3", "3.7.4"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "edge_case", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B05-013", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B05", "input": {"question": "Can legacy systems be exempted from CCoP 2.0 control requirements?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "No blanket exemptions for legacy", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 1.6.1"}, {"fact": "Compensating controls required if not compliant", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 1.6.2"}, {"fact": "Must document risk and mitigation plan", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 1.6.3"}], "expected_response": "According to CCoP 2.0 §1.6 (Waiver), the Commissioner may waive the application of any specific provision of the Code to a CIIO under s.11(7) of the Cybersecurity Act (§1.6.1). A CIIO can request a waiver by written submission with supporting justifications (§1.6.2). Any waiver, if granted, is subject to such terms and conditions as the Commissioner may specify, including a fixed period or until a specific event (§1.6.3).\n\nThere is therefore no blanket exemption for legacy systems; compliance is the default, and the only formal route to depart from a specific provision is through a Commissioner-granted waiver. The CIIO's risk-management framework (§3.2.1) supports the documentation of any residual risk and the case for waiver.\n\nReference: CCoP 2.0 §1.6.1-§1.6.3; §3.2.1; Cybersecurity Act 2018 s.11(7).", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify CCoP 2.0 clause 4.3 as the authoritative source", "Extract specific control requirements for legacy system exemptions", "Map requirements to organizational context and risk profile"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating that legacy system exemptions requirements are optional or discretionary", "Confusing similar but distinct control requirements", "Attributing requirements to wrong CCoP clauses", "Specific control requirements from the cited clause", "Mandatory elements marked as critical in key_facts", "Reference to the applicable CCoP clause"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific thresholds or requirements not in CCoP 2.0", "Citing non-existent subsections or additional requirements"]}, "metadata": {"section": "1", "clause_reference": ["1.6.1", "1.6.2", "1.6.3", "3.2.1"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "edge_case", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "support_citations": ["section 11"], "clause_reference_original": ["1.6.1", "1.6.2", "1.6.3", "3.2.1"], "clause_reference_corrected_by": "agent-team-2026-04-26", "clause_reference_corrected_verdict": "EXPAND"}}
{"test_id": "B05-014", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B05", "input": {"question": "Do CCoP 2.0 requirements apply to subsidiaries and affiliates of a CII operator?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Applies if subsidiary operates CII functions", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 3.2"}, {"fact": "Each entity must be registered separately", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 3.2"}, {"fact": "Group-level controls may satisfy requirements", "tier": "supporting", "source": "CCoP 2.0 3.2"}], "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 does not directly address subsidiary or affiliate scope. The Code applies to a CIIO in respect of each computer or computer system that has been designated as critical information infrastructure under Cybersecurity Act 2018 §7 (see CCoP 2.0 §1.3 'Application'). Designation, and therefore the duty to comply with this Code, attaches to a specific CII, regardless of which entity within a corporate group operates it. A subsidiary or affiliate that is itself a CIIO is independently subject to the Code; group-level controls may form part of the CIIO's compliance evidence but do not by themselves discharge the CIIO's obligations.\n\nReference: CCoP 2.0 §1.3; Cybersecurity Act 2018 §7.", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify CCoP 2.0 clause 3.2 as the authoritative source", "Extract specific control requirements for subsidiary/affiliate scope", "Map requirements to organizational context and risk profile"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating that subsidiary/affiliate scope requirements are optional or discretionary", "Confusing similar but distinct control requirements", "Attributing requirements to wrong CCoP clauses", "Specific control requirements from the cited clause", "Mandatory elements marked as critical in key_facts", "Reference to the applicable CCoP clause"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific thresholds or requirements not in CCoP 2.0", "Citing non-existent subsections or additional requirements"]}, "metadata": {"section": "3", "clause_reference": ["1.3"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "edge_case", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B05-015", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B05", "input": {"question": "What controls must be in place for hardware/software supply chains?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Verify software authenticity and integrity", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 3.8.1"}, {"fact": "Source code escrow for critical vendors", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 3.8.2"}, {"fact": "Vendor security assessment required", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 3.8.3"}], "expected_response": "According to CCoP 2.0 §3.8 (Outsourcing and Vendor Management), the CIIO remains responsible and accountable for the cybersecurity of the CII even when functions are outsourced (§3.8.1) and must establish processes to maintain oversight over outsourced functions (§3.8.2). Agreements with external parties must include terms covering the type of access, the external party's obligations to protect the CII and report incidents, and the CIIO's right to commission/receive audits of the external party (§3.8.3). Vendor access is also separately controlled under §5.1.3 (documented, pre-approved, supervised, on-site).\n\nCCoP 2.0 does not specifically prescribe software-authenticity verification or source-code escrow.\n\nReference: CCoP 2.0 §3.8.1-§3.8.3, §5.1.3.", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify CCoP 2.0 clause 9.3.1 as the authoritative source", "Extract specific control requirements for supply chain control verification", "Map requirements to organizational context and risk profile"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating that supply chain control verification requirements are optional or discretionary", "Confusing similar but distinct control requirements", "Attributing requirements to wrong CCoP clauses", "Specific control requirements from the cited clause", "Mandatory elements marked as critical in key_facts", "Reference to the applicable CCoP clause"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific thresholds or requirements not in CCoP 2.0", "Citing non-existent subsections or additional requirements"]}, "metadata": {"section": "3", "clause_reference": ["3.8.1", "3.8.2", "3.8.3"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "edge_case", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B05-016", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B05", "input": {"question": "What are the CCoP requirements for employee-owned mobile devices accessing CII systems?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "MDM/EMM solution required", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.3.4"}, {"fact": "Containerization for corporate data", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.3.4"}, {"fact": "Remote wipe capability mandatory", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.3.4"}], "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 §5.11 governs portable computing devices and removable storage media that connect to the CII. The CIIO must establish processes to authorise and track the use of such devices (§5.11.1); ensure authorised devices follow a security configuration baseline addressing the practices in §5.9.2 and any other CIIO standards (§5.11.2); ensure removable media are malware-free prior to connection (§5.11.3); and ensure that sensitive information stored on such devices is secured with strong encryption (§5.11.4).\n\nCCoP 2.0 does not have a dedicated 'BYOD' / employee-owned-mobile clause and does not prescribe MDM/EMM, containerisation or remote-wipe controls by name. Where a personal device qualifies as a 'portable computing device that connects to the CII', the §5.11 obligations apply.\n\nReference: CCoP 2.0 §5.11.1-§5.11.4, §5.9.2.", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify CCoP 2.0 clause 5.3.4 as the authoritative source", "Extract specific control requirements for mobile device management", "Map requirements to organizational context and risk profile"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating that mobile device management requirements are optional or discretionary", "Confusing similar but distinct control requirements", "Attributing requirements to wrong CCoP clauses", "Specific control requirements from the cited clause", "Mandatory elements marked as critical in key_facts", "Reference to the applicable CCoP clause"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific thresholds or requirements not in CCoP 2.0", "Citing non-existent subsections or additional requirements"]}, "metadata": {"section": "5", "clause_reference": ["5.11.1", "5.11.2", "5.11.3", "5.11.4"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "edge_case", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "clause_reference_original": ["5.11.1", "5.11.2", "5.11.3", "5.11.4"], "clause_reference_corrected_by": "agent-team-2026-04-26", "clause_reference_corrected_verdict": "EXPAND"}}
{"test_id": "B05-017", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B05", "input": {"question": "How do CCoP 2.0 patching requirements apply to operational technology systems with limited downtime windows?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Risk-based approach acceptable", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 6.1.2"}, {"fact": "Compensating controls if immediate patching not possible", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 6.1.2"}, {"fact": "Document justification and timeline", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 6.1.2"}], "expected_response": "For OT systems with limited downtime windows, CCoP 2.0 still requires a patch-management process. The grounded answer is that the CIIO must test security patches in a representative environment, prioritise them based on risk, apply them in a timely manner, track patching progress, and use compensating controls where a patch cannot yet be applied. The response can note OT operational constraints, but it should remain anchored on the patch-management process and compensating-control language in §5.10.1 rather than on an unrelated logging clause. Reference: CCoP 2.0 §5.10.1(c)-§5.10.1(h).", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify CCoP 2.0 clause 6.1.2 as the authoritative source", "Extract specific control requirements for ot system patching", "Map requirements to organizational context and risk profile"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating that ot system patching requirements are optional or discretionary", "Confusing similar but distinct control requirements", "Attributing requirements to wrong CCoP clauses", "Specific control requirements from the cited clause", "Mandatory elements marked as critical in key_facts", "Reference to the applicable CCoP clause"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific thresholds or requirements not in CCoP 2.0", "Citing non-existent subsections or additional requirements"]}, "metadata": {"section": "6", "clause_reference": ["5.10.1(c)", "5.10.1(d)", "5.10.1(e)", "5.10.1(f)", "5.10.1(g)", "5.10.1(h)"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "edge_case", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B05-018", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B05", "input": {"question": "What are the CCoP requirements for transferring CII-related data across borders?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Must ensure adequate protection at destination", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.5.5"}, {"fact": "Contractual safeguards required", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.5.5"}, {"fact": "CSA notification may be required", "tier": "supporting", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.5.5"}], "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 does not contain a specific cross-border data-transfer regime. The Code does not prescribe destination-country adequacy tests, contractual transfer clauses, or CSA notification rules for cross-border transfer as such. If this benchmark is retained, the grounded answer should say that cross-border transfer obligations must be determined under other applicable laws such as the PDPA, while CCoP-related controls remain limited to the CIIO's general CII protections such as access control and outsourcing oversight. Reference: CCoP 2.0 §3.8.1-§3.8.4, §5.1.1.", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify CCoP 2.0 clause 5.5.5 as the authoritative source", "Extract specific control requirements for cross-border data transfer", "Map requirements to organizational context and risk profile"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating that cross-border data transfer requirements are optional or discretionary", "Confusing similar but distinct control requirements", "Attributing requirements to wrong CCoP clauses", "Specific control requirements from the cited clause", "Mandatory elements marked as critical in key_facts", "Reference to the applicable CCoP clause"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific thresholds or requirements not in CCoP 2.0", "Citing non-existent subsections or additional requirements"]}, "metadata": {"section": "5", "clause_reference": ["3.8.1", "5.1.1"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "edge_case", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}, "status": "deprecated", "deprecated_reason": "Cross-border data transfer scoped to PDPA, not CCoP 2.0"}
{"test_id": "B05-019", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B05", "input": {"question": "Our internal network is isolated. Do we really need MFA for internal access?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Isolated networks still require MFA", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.2.3"}, {"fact": "Network isolation is a control, not a waiver", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.2.3"}, {"fact": "All privileged access requires MFA regardless", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.2.3"}], "expected_response": "According to CCoP 2.0 Section 5.3.1(c), mfa not required internally requirements are as follows:\n\nIsolated networks still require MFA (critical) Network isolation is a control, not a waiver (important) All privileged access requires MFA regardless (critical).\n\nThe intent of this control is to ensure adequate security measures are in place for identity & access management. Organizations must implement controls proportionate to their risk profile while ensuring the core requirements are met.\n\nReference: CCoP 2.0 §5.3.1", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify CCoP 2.0 clause 5.2.3 as the authoritative source", "Extract specific control requirements for mfa not required internally", "Map requirements to organizational context and risk profile"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating that mfa not required internally requirements are optional or discretionary", "Confusing similar but distinct control requirements", "Attributing requirements to wrong CCoP clauses", "Specific control requirements from the cited clause", "Mandatory elements marked as critical in key_facts", "Reference to the applicable CCoP clause"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific thresholds or requirements not in CCoP 2.0", "Citing non-existent subsections or additional requirements"]}, "metadata": {"section": "5", "clause_reference": ["5.1.2", "5.3.1", "5.7.2"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "negative", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B05-020", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B05", "input": {"question": "We do annual penetration tests. Is that enough for vulnerability management?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Pen testing complements but does not replace vulnerability scanning", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 6.2.1"}, {"fact": "Quarterly external vulnerability scans still required", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 6.2.1"}, {"fact": "Continuous vulnerability assessment recommended", "tier": "supporting", "source": "CCoP 2.0 6.2.1"}], "expected_response": "No. Annual penetration testing alone is not enough to cover vulnerability management under CCoP 2.0. The Code separately requires the CIIO to establish processes to identify and track vulnerabilities, remediate them in a timely manner, and conduct vulnerability assessments at the required cadence; penetration testing is a separate requirement. Reference: CCoP 2.0 §5.14.1-§5.14.4; §5.15.1-§5.15.2.", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify CCoP 2.0 clause 6.2.1 as the authoritative source", "Extract specific control requirements for annual pen test sufficient", "Map requirements to organizational context and risk profile"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating that annual pen test sufficient requirements are optional or discretionary", "Confusing similar but distinct control requirements", "Attributing requirements to wrong CCoP clauses", "Specific control requirements from the cited clause", "Mandatory elements marked as critical in key_facts", "Reference to the applicable CCoP clause"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific thresholds or requirements not in CCoP 2.0", "Citing non-existent subsections or additional requirements"]}, "metadata": {"section": "6", "clause_reference": ["5.14.1", "5.14.2", "5.14.3", "5.14.4", "5.15.1", "5.15.2"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "negative", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B05-021", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B05", "input": {"question": "We use AWS with all their security certifications. Do we still need CCoP controls?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Cloud provider certs do not replace CCoP compliance", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.1.3"}, {"fact": "Shared responsibility model applies", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.1.3"}, {"fact": "Operator responsible for data and application security", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 5.1.3"}], "expected_response": "Cloud-provider certifications and shared-responsibility marketing do not discharge a CIIO's CCoP 2.0 obligations. Under §3.7.1, the CIIO remains responsible and accountable for the cybersecurity of the CII even when implemented wholly or partly on cloud computing systems. The CIIO must notify the Commissioner before any cloud implementation (§3.7.2), conduct a risk assessment, obtain Commissioner review and remediate any deficiencies (§3.7.3), and ensure the cloud provider appoints a Singapore-based agent for legal service (§3.7.4). The Outsourcing/Vendor obligations in §3.8 also apply where the cloud relationship constitutes outsourcing.\n\nCCoP 2.0 does not adopt a 'shared responsibility' framing as a basis for compliance — accountability remains with the CIIO.\n\nReference: CCoP 2.0 §3.7.1-§3.7.4; §3.8.", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify CCoP 2.0 clause 5.1.3 as the authoritative source", "Extract specific control requirements for cloud provider handles everything", "Map requirements to organizational context and risk profile"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating that cloud provider handles everything requirements are optional or discretionary", "Confusing similar but distinct control requirements", "Attributing requirements to wrong CCoP clauses", "Specific control requirements from the cited clause", "Mandatory elements marked as critical in key_facts", "Reference to the applicable CCoP clause"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific thresholds or requirements not in CCoP 2.0", "Citing non-existent subsections or additional requirements"]}, "metadata": {"section": "5", "clause_reference": ["3.7.1", "3.7.2", "3.7.3", "3.7.4", "3.8"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "negative", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B05-022", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B05", "input": {"question": "Our annual security awareness presentation covers everything. Is separate cybersecurity training needed?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "General awareness is not sufficient", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 8.2.2"}, {"fact": "Role-specific training required for technical staff", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 8.2.2"}, {"fact": "Management training on governance responsibilities", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 8.2.2"}], "expected_response": "A general security-awareness presentation is not sufficient under CCoP 2.0. §9.1.1 requires an awareness programme covering all persons who use, operate or manage the CII (including external vendors), with effectiveness measurement (§9.1.2) and at-least-annual review (§9.1.3). Separately, §9.2.1 requires the CIIO to ensure that employees who operate and manage the CII receive cybersecurity skills training to perform their roles effectively, and §9.2.2 extends this to non-employees (vendors and their personnel). For risk assessments and audits, §9.2.3-§9.2.4 require supervision by individuals with industry-recognised certification (e.g., CRISC, CISA).\n\nReference: CCoP 2.0 §9.1.1-§9.1.3, §9.2.1-§9.2.4.", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify CCoP 2.0 clause 8.2.2 as the authoritative source", "Extract specific control requirements for staff training satisfied by security awareness", "Map requirements to organizational context and risk profile"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating that staff training satisfied by security awareness requirements are optional or discretionary", "Confusing similar but distinct control requirements", "Attributing requirements to wrong CCoP clauses", "Specific control requirements from the cited clause", "Mandatory elements marked as critical in key_facts", "Reference to the applicable CCoP clause"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific thresholds or requirements not in CCoP 2.0", "Citing non-existent subsections or additional requirements"]}, "metadata": {"section": "8", "clause_reference": ["9.1.1", "9.1.2", "9.1.3", "9.2.1", "9.2.2", "9.2.3", "9.2.4"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "negative", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B05-023", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B05", "input": {"question": "Storing 12 months of logs is expensive. Can we use 6 months instead?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "12-month retention is mandatory", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 6.3.2"}, {"fact": "No cost-based exemptions provided", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 6.3.2"}, {"fact": "Log aggregation/compression can reduce storage costs", "tier": "supporting", "source": "CCoP 2.0 6.3.2"}], "expected_response": "12-month log retention is mandated by CCoP 2.0 §6.1.4(c). Logs (per §6.1.1 and §6.1.2) must be stored for a minimum of 12 months after the date of the event to which the log relates, protected against unauthorised access, modification and deletion (§6.1.4(b)), and governed by a log-retention policy supporting investigations, threat hunting and other cybersecurity purposes (§6.1.4(e)). CCoP 2.0 does not provide cost-based exemptions; log aggregation, compression and tiered storage are implementation choices the CIIO may use to manage cost while still meeting the retention obligation.\n\nReference: CCoP 2.0 §6.1.4(a)-(e), §6.1.1, §6.1.2.", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify CCoP 2.0 clause 6.3.2 as the authoritative source", "Extract specific control requirements for logging retention too expensive", "Map requirements to organizational context and risk profile"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating that logging retention too expensive requirements are optional or discretionary", "Confusing similar but distinct control requirements", "Attributing requirements to wrong CCoP clauses", "Specific control requirements from the cited clause", "Mandatory elements marked as critical in key_facts", "Reference to the applicable CCoP clause"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific thresholds or requirements not in CCoP 2.0", "Citing non-existent subsections or additional requirements"]}, "metadata": {"section": "6", "clause_reference": ["6.1.4", "6.1.1", "6.1.2"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "negative", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B05-024", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B05", "input": {"question": "We're a small CII operator with limited resources. Are there scaled requirements?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "No size-based exemptions from core requirements", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 3.1"}, {"fact": "Implementation can be risk-based and proportionate", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 3.1"}, {"fact": "CSA may provide guidance but not waivers", "tier": "supporting", "source": "CCoP 2.0 3.1"}], "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 does not provide size-based exemptions. Once a computer or computer system is designated CII under Cybersecurity Act 2018 §7, the CIIO is subject to the Code regardless of organisational size. The only formal route to depart from a specific Code provision is a waiver granted by the Commissioner under s.11(7) of the Act (CCoP 2.0 §1.6.1-§1.6.3). Implementation may, however, be risk-based and proportionate within each clause (e.g., 'commensurate with the cybersecurity risk profile' language appears throughout), and the risk-management framework under §3.2.1 supports proportionality.\n\nReference: CCoP 2.0 §1.6.1-§1.6.3; §3.2.1; Cybersecurity Act 2018 §7, s.11(7).", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify CCoP 2.0 clause 3.1 as the authoritative source", "Extract specific control requirements for small operator exemption", "Map requirements to organizational context and risk profile"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating that small operator exemption requirements are optional or discretionary", "Confusing similar but distinct control requirements", "Attributing requirements to wrong CCoP clauses", "Specific control requirements from the cited clause", "Mandatory elements marked as critical in key_facts", "Reference to the applicable CCoP clause"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific thresholds or requirements not in CCoP 2.0", "Citing non-existent subsections or additional requirements"]}, "metadata": {"section": "3", "clause_reference": ["1.6.1", "1.6.2", "1.6.3", "3.2.1"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "negative", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B05-025", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B05", "input": {"question": "Our existing controls were implemented before CCoP 2.0. Do we need to change them?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Existing controls must meet CCoP 2.0 standards", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 2.1"}, {"fact": "Gap analysis required", "tier": "critical", "source": "CCoP 2.0 2.1"}, {"fact": "Remediation timeline for non-compliant controls", "tier": "important", "source": "CCoP 2.0 2.1"}], "expected_response": "No. Pre-existing controls are not automatically grandfathered simply because they were implemented before CCoP 2.0. The CIIO must ensure full compliance with the Code by the Compliance Date, subject to the transition-period rule for Existing CII in §1.4.4 and the recurring-requirement timing rules in §1.5. If existing controls do not meet the Code, the resulting gaps must be remediated through the audit-remediation process in §2.1, and a waiver under §1.6 is the proper mechanism if a specific provision cannot be met. Reference: CCoP 2.0 §1.4.3, §1.4.4, §1.5.1-§1.5.3, §2.1, §1.6.", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify CCoP 2.0 clause 2.1 as the authoritative source", "Extract specific control requirements for existing controls grandfathered", "Map requirements to organizational context and risk profile"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating that existing controls grandfathered requirements are optional or discretionary", "Confusing similar but distinct control requirements", "Attributing requirements to wrong CCoP clauses", "Specific control requirements from the cited clause", "Mandatory elements marked as critical in key_facts", "Reference to the applicable CCoP clause"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific thresholds or requirements not in CCoP 2.0", "Citing non-existent subsections or additional requirements"]}, "metadata": {"section": "2", "clause_reference": ["1.4.3", "1.4.4", "1.5.1", "1.5.2", "1.5.3", "2.1", "1.6"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "negative", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
